1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,990
This piston weighs five and a half tonnes.

2
00:00:02,380 --> 00:00:04,910
The crankshaft it turns
weighs three hundred tonnes.

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:09,390
And the engine it belongs to is
taller than a four-storey building.

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:11,250
But here's the strange part.

5
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:14,690
At full power, it turns only
about a hundred times a minute.

6
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:18,310
Slower than your car engine when
it's just sitting at a red light.

7
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:21,230
Today we're climbing every
level of ship engine,

8
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:24,970
from the little motor on a fishing
boat, all the way up to this monster.

9
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,330
And one of these levels is so powerful,

10
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:29,370
it doesn't burn any fuel at all.

11
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:30,870
Let's start small.

12
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:33,510
This is an outboard motor: an engine,

13
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:37,430
a gearbox and a propeller in one
box, clamped to the back of a boat.

14
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:40,150
The smallest ones make
just a few horsepower.

15
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:42,090
You can carry them with one hand.

16
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:43,850
But outboards have grown up.

17
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:47,710
Mercury's V12 Verado makes six hundred
horsepower from a single motor.

18
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:49,910
It weighs about as much as a small car,

19
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:52,190
and costs more than many of them.

20
00:00:52,380 --> 00:00:55,170
Fast boats hang two, three,
even four of these on the back.

21
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:57,260
Four of them is two thousand four

22
00:00:57,310 --> 00:00:59,470
hundred horsepower on one small boat.

23
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:02,210
But even six hundred
horsepower is tiny compared

24
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:04,270
to what you need to push something heavy.

25
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:06,430
Remember the tugboats from the harbour?

26
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:09,930
They're small, but they
have one job: push and pull

27
00:01:09,980 --> 00:01:12,490
ships that weigh a hundred
times more than they do.

28
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:16,850
A modern harbour tug like PSA Thor
in Singapore has two diesel engines,

29
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,730
each making about two and
a half thousand kilowatts.

30
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:21,710
Together, that's nearly seven thousand

31
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:24,410
horsepower in a boat you
could park in a car park.

32
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:26,210
The clever part is underneath.

33
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:29,630
Its propellers sit in drives
that can spin all the way around,

34
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:33,010
so the tug can push in any
direction, even sideways.

35
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:37,050
Its pulling force, called bollard
pull, is about eighty tonnes.

36
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,830
That's like a rope tied to forty
cars being dragged at once.

37
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:44,790
Next, the engines that run
ferries, offshore ships and the

38
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:48,950
power systems of big vessels:
medium-speed four-stroke diesels.

39
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:51,690
They work like the engine in
a truck, just much bigger,

40
00:01:51,740 --> 00:01:54,690
with each cylinder as
big as a washing machine.

41
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:59,350
In 2015, one of them, the Wärtsilä
31, set a Guinness World Record

42
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:03,110
as the most fuel-efficient four-stroke
diesel engine in the world.

43
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:04,550
Depending on the model,

44
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:07,870
one engine makes between
about four and ten megawatts.

45
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:10,050
That's enough to power thousands of homes.

46
00:02:10,340 --> 00:02:11,570
Now things get fast.

47
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:14,670
Many warships don't use a
normal ship engine at all.

48
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:15,830
They use a jet engine.

49
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:20,350
The General Electric LM2500
was developed from the CF6,

50
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:22,890
an engine that powers big airliners.

51
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:25,970
Instead of pushing a plane,
it spins a propeller shaft.

52
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:30,070
One LM2500 makes about
thirty-three thousand horsepower,

53
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:32,150
and it weighs less than five tonnes.

54
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:34,710
That's a huge amount
of power for its size.

55
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:38,210
An American Arleigh Burke-class
destroyer carries four of them.

56
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:41,070
And by 2004, more than thirty navies had

57
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:43,110
used over a thousand of these turbines.

58
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:44,370
The downside?

59
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:45,790
Jet engines drink fuel.

60
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,850
They're perfect for speed,
terrible for long, cheap trips.

61
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:52,590
That's why you'll almost never
find one in a cargo ship,

62
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:54,950
where every litre of fuel is money.

63
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:57,070
The world's biggest cruise ship,

64
00:02:57,120 --> 00:02:59,850
Icon of the Seas, doesn't
have one big engine.

65
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:01,290
It has six.

66
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:04,890
They burn liquefied natural gas
or diesel, and together they make

67
00:03:04,940 --> 00:03:08,710
about sixty-seven megawatts,
roughly ninety thousand horsepower.

68
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,010
But none of them is
connected to a propeller.

69
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:13,610
They make electricity,
like a power station.

70
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:16,790
That electricity runs the lights,
the pools, the restaurants,

71
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:21,050
and giant electric motors in pods under
the ship that turn the propellers.

72
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:23,630
So the biggest cruise ship
in the world is really

73
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:26,390
a floating power station
with a hotel on top.

74
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:29,070
Remember the level that doesn't burn fuel?

75
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:30,250
This is it.

76
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:34,070
A Nimitz-class aircraft carrier is
powered by two nuclear reactors.

77
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:36,550
Each one makes steam that drives turbines,

78
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:38,922
and together they send
around two hundred and eighty

79
00:03:38,972 --> 00:03:41,670
thousand horsepower to
four giant propellers.

80
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:44,330
And they don't need to
refuel every few weeks.

81
00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:46,810
The reactors are built
to run for about twenty

82
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:49,690
to twenty-five years
before they get new fuel.

83
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:52,530
That's the most powerful
ship propulsion on this list.

84
00:03:52,580 --> 00:03:54,070
But it's not one engine.

85
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:56,410
It's two reactors and a
whole plant of turbines.

86
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:58,750
Which brings us to the final level: the

87
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:01,550
biggest single engine
ever put inside a ship.

88
00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:05,590
This is the Wärtsilä-Sulzer RT-flex96C.

89
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:07,630
In its fourteen-cylinder version,

90
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:10,690
it's the largest diesel
engine of its kind ever built.

91
00:04:10,820 --> 00:04:15,070
It's thirteen and a half metres tall,
almost twenty-seven metres long,

92
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:18,090
and it weighs more than two
thousand three hundred tonnes.

93
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:21,070
Each cylinder is ninety-six
centimetres wide.

94
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:23,850
Each piston moves two and
a half metres up and down.

95
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:25,870
You could stand inside
one of the cylinders.

96
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:28,350
The piston from the start of this video?

97
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:30,930
Together with its rod,
it's six metres tall.

98
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:34,470
And the crankshaft, the part
that turns all that up-and-down

99
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:37,610
motion into spinning,
weighs three hundred tonnes.

100
00:04:37,940 --> 00:04:39,890
That's as heavy as about two hundred cars.

101
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:42,842
Together, the fourteen
cylinders make about one

102
00:04:42,892 --> 00:04:46,150
hundred and seven thousand
horsepower, from one engine.

103
00:04:46,220 --> 00:04:48,310
This engine was built for the Emma Maersk,

104
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:51,010
a container ship almost
four hundred metres long.

105
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:53,163
At full power, it burns about fourteen

106
00:04:53,213 --> 00:04:55,730
thousand litres of heavy
fuel oil every hour.

107
00:04:55,780 --> 00:04:57,710
And remember how slowly it turns?

108
00:04:58,200 --> 00:05:00,250
About a hundred revolutions a minute.

109
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:01,570
That's not a weakness.

110
00:05:01,960 --> 00:05:03,010
It's the whole point.

111
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:06,730
A giant propeller works best when
it turns slowly, so this engine

112
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:10,870
is connected straight to the propeller
shaft, with no gearbox at all.

113
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:14,230
Slow, huge, and simple: that's why engines

114
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:16,530
like this push most of the world's cargo.

115
00:05:17,020 --> 00:05:19,114
So from a six-hundred-horsepower
outboard to

116
00:05:19,164 --> 00:05:21,510
a two-thousand-three-hundred-tonne engine,

117
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:23,850
every level of ship has
the engine that fits

118
00:05:23,900 --> 00:05:29,170
its job: speed, pulling power,
electricity, endurance, or pure size.

119
00:05:29,220 --> 00:05:30,490
Here's one last comparison.

120
00:05:30,820 --> 00:05:33,750
Seawise Giant, the
longest ship ever built,

121
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:36,670
had a steam turbine of about
fifty thousand horsepower.

122
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:39,010
This one engine makes
more than twice that.

123
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:41,470
If you want to see what happens
when an engine like this

124
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:45,170
suddenly stops in the middle of
the ocean, watch this video next.
